no version yet, pnpm records the running pnpm if it satisfies the range and was published at least minimumReleaseAge ago. Otherwise pnpm records the newest version in the range that is old enough. If no version in the range is old enough, pnpm records the running pnpm.
{
  "devEngines": {
    "packageManager": {
      "name": "pnpm",
      "version": ">=12.0.0 <13.0.0",
      "onFail": "download"
    }
  }
}

Note
When pnpm 12 is declared via the legacy packageManager field and pmOnFail is not set to ignore, its resolution info is written to pnpm-lock.yaml. The lockfile then gains a leading env lockfile document.

hat $PNPM_HOME/bin is on your PATH.
You can check the current global bin directory with:
pnpm bin -g

Project-aware global bins#
Added in: v12.0.0-rc.2 (pnpm v12 only)
Global commands can follow the project you are standing in. When you run a global command from inside a project that asks for a different version of the same tool, pnpm runs the version the project asks for.
The most useful case is Node.js itself. Given a project that pins its runtime:
package.json{
  "devEngines": {
    "runtime": {
      "name": "node",
      "version": "^22.0.0",
      "onFail": "download"
    }
  }
}

